In essential, the local chemical environment around the active site plays a decisive role in mass transport and charge transfer [23, 24]. Acceptably, the two-dimensional nanosheets and small-sized nanostructure offered more active sites and spacious mass transport area [25]. In another aspect, the uniform nano-sized materials loaded on or encapsulated in conductive substrates, can promote charge transfer and optimize the activity of catalytic sites [26, 27]. In some special structures, such as core-shell or chain mail, the substrate can not only serve as a "transfer station" to accelerate the efficiency of charge transfer and mass transport, but also can be used as "armor" to keep the structure of active materials stable [28, 29]. Thus, the construction of small sized nanoparticles encapsulated in conductive carbon nanosheets for improve the mass transport and charge transfer in OER process would be a rational approach to develop highly active and stable sulfide compounds electrocatalysts. Additionally, heteroatom-doped can modulate the d-band center and the local electron density [30, 31]. The movement of the d-band center and change of the local electron density can efficiently regulate the interaction between
active site and intermediates to enhance the intrinsic activity. Banking on the above analysis, it is instructive to integrate "atoms doped-carbon layer sheet encapsulated-interspace rich" into a catalytic system for the synergistic regulation of the intrinsic activities, mass transport and structure stability on OER process.
Inspired by the atomic dispersion metal sites and thermal instability of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), herein, encapsulating \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles within S, N co-incorporated carbon nanosheets were proposed and obtained via fast thermal treatment from ultrathin MOFs nanosheets. The d-band center of the \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles was modulated by Fe doping, which shift away from the fermi level, suggesting that the Fe doping facilitates desorption of oxygen intermediates from \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\). Meanwhile, the dense, uniform and small-sized Fe-dope \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles anchored S, N co-incorporated carbon substrates morphology was realized, which can deliver more active area, fluent mass transfer channel and sufficient intrinsic activity. As a result, the optimized catalysts present an ultralow overpotential of \(273~\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10~\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) and small Tafel slope of \(55.8\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\), which is much better than most of Co-based electrocatalysts and even close to the benchmark \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) catalyst.
## 2 Experimental section
## 2.1 Synthesis of Co-MOFs and Fe doped Co-MOFs nanosheets
The CoFe-MOFs was obtained through a typical synthesis method [13, 32], \(0.0592\,\mathrm{g}\) of 4,4-bipyridine \((4,4^{\prime}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Bpy})\), 0.0560 g of 2,5-thiophenedicarboxylic acid (Tdc) and \(0.0264\mathrm{~g~NaOH}\) were dissolved in \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) of deionized water by ultrasonication treatment. After completely dissolving, added \(0.010\ \mathrm{g}\) dimethyldioctadecylammonium bromide (DDAB) to the above mixture and ultrasonic for \(5\;\mathrm{min}\). Then, \(2~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(0.1\;\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) the mixture solution consisted of \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in a 9:1 ratio was quickly added to the above mixed solution. After stir treatment for \(10\,\mathrm{\min}\), the resultant dispersions were centrifuged to collect the product. Subsequently the precipitate was washed with deionized water, and then dried at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in vacuum for \(6\,\mathrm{{h}}\). The synthesized process of Co-MOFs was the same as the CoFe-MOFs, except that \(2~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\) the mixture solution consisted of \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in a 9:1 ratio was replaced by \(2~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(0.1\,\mathrm{~M~CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\) for the Co-MOFs nanosheets, respectively.
## 2.2 Synthesis of \(\mathbf{CoS}_{8}@\mathbf{SNC}\) and Fe- \(\mathbf{Co}_{9}\mathbf{S}_{8}@\mathbf{SNC}\)
The prepared Fe- \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) nanosheets were synthesized by fast thermal treatment topological transformation of CoFe-MOFs. In detail, CoFe-MOFs was used as precursor to heat-treat for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) on the furnace with a temperature of \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in Ar atmosphere. During this process, CoFe-MOFs were pyrolysis and the metal-O coordination transformed to metal-S coordination. Meanwhile, the morphology of CoFe-MOFs was preserved as the substrate to anchor Fe-doped \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles. Co-MOFs were used as precursor to obtain \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\). Then, the thermal treatment process was similar to the synthesis Fe- \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\).
## 2.3 Characterizations
The as-prepared samples were characterized by X-ray diffractometer (D2 PHASER, Bruker) with a Cu radiation source \((\lambda=\) \(1.5405\,\mathrm{\AA})\). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed using an AXIS-ULTRA D-600W instrument using C 1s
(binding energy of \(285~\mathrm{eV}\) ) as the reference. The morphology and microstructure of the as-prepared samples were examined by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, FEI quanta650) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM, FEI Tecnai G2 F20 S-TWIN). Raman spectrum was studied on a laser confocal Raman spectroscopy (WITec Alpha300 Raman) using a \(532\;\mathrm{nm}\) excitation laser.
## 2.4 Density functional theory (DFT) calculations
All the calculations were performed within the framework of the DFT as implemented in the Vienna ab initio software package (VASP 5.3.5) code within the Perdew–Burke–Ernzerhof (PBE) generalized gradient approximation and the projected augmented wave (PAW) method [33–36]. The cutoff energy for the plane-wave basis set was set to \(450~\mathrm{eV}\). The Brillouin zone of the surface unit cell was sampled by Monkhorst–Pack (MP) grids for \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) structure optimizations [37]. A \(2\times2\) supercell of the \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) (222) surface including 8 atomic layers were constructed to model the catalyst in this work. The \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) surfaces were determined by \(3\times3\times1\) Monkhorst–Pack grid. The convergence criterion for the electronic self-consistent iteration and force was set to \(10^{-5}\ \mathrm{eV}\) and \(0.01\ \mathrm{eV}{\cdot}\mathrm{\AA}^{-1}\), respectively. A vacuum layer of \(12\mathrm{~\AA~}\) was introduced to avoid interactions between periodic images.
## 2.5 Electrochemical measurements
Electrochemical measurements were performed in a standard three-electrode system controlled by a CHI 760E potentiostat (CH instrument, Shanghai, China). The working electrode was a glassy carbon electrode of \(5\;\mathrm{mm}\) in diameter. The reference and counter electrodes are \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) and graphite rod, respectively. In 1 M KOH, the potential of the \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) was corrected relative to reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). The calibration was performed in a \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) saturated electrolyte with a Pt wire as the working electrode. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) was run at a scan rate of \(1\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\), and the average of the two potentials at which the current crossed zero, which was \(-1.003\;\mathrm{V}\). So in 1 M KOH
\[
E\left(\mathrm{RHE}\right)=E\left(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\right)+1.003\mathrm{V}
\]
Typically, \(5~\mathrm{mg}\) of catalyst powder dispersed in a mixture solution consisting of \(975\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of ethanol \((95\%)\) and \(25\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(5\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) Nafion solution, and ultrasonic to form a uniform solution. Then, \(20~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of the ink was dropped onto a glassy carbon electrode. The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves were measured at a scan rate of \(5\;\mathrm{\mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\). The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) tests were measured over a frequency range from \(10^{5}\) to \(10^{-1}\,\mathrm{Hz}\) at a potential of \(1.5\;\mathrm{V}\).
For temperature-dependent measurements, a sealed glass cell was suspended in a thermostatic water bath. The apparent electrochemical activation energy \(\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)\) for OER can be determined using the Arrhenius relationship [1, 38]
\[
\frac{\partial(\log i_{\mathrm{k}})}{\partial(1/T)}\bigg|_{\eta}=\frac{E_{\mathrm{a}}}{2.3R}
\]
where \(i_{\mathrm{k}}\) is the kinetic current at a potential of \(1.55\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE at different temperatures, \(T\) is the temperature, and \(R\) is the universal gas constant. The Arrhenius plots at a potential of \(1.55\;\mathrm{V}\) for different catalysts were obtained. From the slop of the Arrhenius plots, the apparent electrochemical activation energy can be extracted.
## 3 Results and discussion
3 Results and discussionThe dense Fe-doped \(\mathrm{CoS}_{8}\) nanoparticles encapsulated by S, N co-incorporated carbon nanosheets (denoted as Fe- \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\)) nanosheets were prepared by a facile self-assembly process with the assistance of surfactant at room temperature [13]. In the following steps, a fast thermal treatment in Ar atmosphere were applied to convert CoFe-MOFs into Fe- \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\). As a contrast, the \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) was prepared by a similar method from fast thermal treatment from Co-MOFs nanosheets without Fe-doping. The composition and morphology of as prepared samples were confirmed by the X-ray diffraction (XRD), SEM and transmission electron microscopy (TEM). The XRD patterns of CoFe-MOFs and Co-MOFs present anisotropic growth of the synthesized MOFs nanosheets (Fig. S1 in the ESM), and all the characteristic peak are well indexed to the previously reported Co-MOFs [39]. Compared with Co-MOFs, no obvious new diffraction peak was appeared in CoFe-MOFs, suggesting that the Fe was successfully doping to the Co-MOFs frameworks. The SEM image and element mappings show that the morphology of the synthesized MOFs are ultrathin nanosheets (Figs. S2–S4 in the ESM). Subsequently, the synthesized MOFs nanosheets transformed into Fe- \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) (Figs. S5 and S6 in the ESM) by the fast thermal treatment in Ar atmosphere, respectively. Expectedly, Fe- \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) inherit the sheet-like morphology of MOFs precursors (Figs. S7 and S8 in the ESM). Meanwhile, the HRTEM images exhibit the small uniform size nanoparticles are encapsulated by the S, N co-incorporated carbon layer to form nanosheets (Fig. 1). This nanosheet by connected nanoparticles retained rich channel for the permeation of electrolyte and the release of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) bubbles during OER process [12]. Beyond that, carbon layer are about 2–3 layers, which ensures the smooth mass migration to the catalyst at the same time play the role of armor protection. Moreover, these nanoparticles presented the average size of Fe- \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\)
and \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) are 8.76 and \(9.04~\mathrm{nm}\) by statistical analysis, respectively (Fig. S9 in the ESM). The small uniform size distribution is due to discrete metal sites in the MOFs precursors. For electrocatalytic process, the small size ensures that the particles uniformly distributed on the S, N co-incorporated in carbon nanosheets, and provide more active sites. Furthermore, the internal nanoparticles detected the interplanar spacing with 0.249 and \(0.202\;\mathrm{nm}\) , corresponding to the (400) and (422) planes of \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) (Figs. 1(d) and \(1(\mathrm{g})\) ), respectively. The XRD patterns of Fe- \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) show the formation of the cubic \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) crystal phase (Fig. 2(a)). In addition, the Raman peaks of 188.7 and \(665.9~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) were detected in Fe\(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) (Fig. 2(b)), which are corresponding to the characteristic peaks of \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) [40, 41]. The vibration peaks of 1,552 and \(1,993~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) means that the carbon layer is graphitic carbon structure (Fig. S10 in the ESM) [17, 28]. Therefore, these results prove that the dense, small Fe-doped \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles are encapsulated by the S, N co-incorporated graphitic carbon layers and form nanosheets via self-assembly. Noticeably, due to the atomic dispersion and thermal instability of MOFs, the fast thermal treatment topological transformation strategy was proposed for obtaining graphitic carbon encapsulating nanoparticles materials.
To identify the chemical environment in Fe-doped \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\), XPS was conducted. As show in Fig. 2(c), the Co 2p of Fe\(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) exhibited two main peaks of \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) (778.5 eV) and \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) (793.7 eV), and the spectrums present asymmetric core electron peaks, which were the characteristic peaks of \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) [42]. The Fe 2p has a similar weak peak, suggesting Fe successfully doped to \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) (Fig. S11 in the ESM). Meanwhile, Co 2p of Fe- \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) has a positive shift of \(0.3~\mathrm{eV}\) compared to the \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) (Fig. S12(a) in the ESM), indicating the local electron structure of Co atom has been regulated by incorporating Fe into \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) [12, 13, 30]. Especially, the Co 2p peaks of Fe- \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) can be further deconvoluted. 778.5, 779.7 and \(782.3~\mathrm{eV}\) originate from the
Co-Co, Co-S and Co-N bond, respectively [17]. And \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) has a significantly increased percentage composition of the Co-S bond (Table S1 in the ESM), suggesting more of the formation Co octahedral coordination in \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) than \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\). Thus, Fe doping can tune the formation proportion of the Co tetrahedral and octahedral coordinated by S in \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\). Reportedly, the hexa-coordinated Co possess more efficient ability for the adsorption of reaction radicals in \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) during the OER process in the alkaline condition [30]. Moreover, the satellites peaks at 786.1 and \(802.7\ \mathrm{eV}\) indicate the coexistence of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) [14]. In the S 2p XPS spectrum (Fig. 2(d)), the peaks at 162.4, 163.7, and \(165.0\ \mathrm{eV}\) are attributed to the Co–S, C–S–C and N, S–C bond [43], respectively. The peak of the N, S-C bond has an apparently increased with the Fe doping in the \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) (Table S2 in the ESM), suggesting that the enhanced electron interaction between S and N atom. Moreover, it is also obvious that a similar positive shift of \(0.3\ \mathrm{eV}\) for S 2p after the Fe doping in the \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) (Fig. S12(b) in the ESM). For the C 1s, the C–C bond, C–N bond and C–S bond were co-existing, which further demonstrate the S and N co-incorporating in carbon layer (Fig. 2(e)). What's more, the enhanced peaks of the C–S bond suggested that Fe doping strengthens the interaction between \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) and the carbon layer (Table S3 in the ESM). In addition, the N 1s XPS reveals that \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) has a higher proportion of graphitic N \((82.3\%)\) than \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) \((65.8\%)\) (Fig. 2(f) and Table S4 in the ESM), further indicating N doping in the carbon layer and the carbon layer was graphitic carbon [17]. The high doping proportion of graphitic N in the \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) may improve the OER electrocatalytic activity of active sites [44, 45]. Apparently, \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) has a higher proportion of N and S than \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) (Table S5 in the ESM), which means that the Fe doping can facilitate the incorporation of S and N into the carbon nanosheets. As a result, \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) possess high doping proportion of N-doping graphitic carbon and the enhanced interaction between \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) and the carbon layer than \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\). According to the analysis above, Fe incorporation can effectively modulate the coordination of Co atom in \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) and enhance the interaction between \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles and the graphitic carbon layer.
In order to investigate the influence of Fe-doping \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles on water electro-oxidation activity, \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\), \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) and commercial \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\), were used as electrocatalysts
in \(1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH}\) solution. The \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) reference electrode was corrected relative to a reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) (Fig. S13 in the ESM). As expected, \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) exhibit faster current density response than \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) with the increase of potential, and exceeds the performance of commercial \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) at a higher overpotential than \(290\ \mathrm{mV}\) (Fig. 3(a)). Specifically, \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) only need an overpotential of \(273\ \mathrm{mV}\) to drive \(10\ \mathrm{mA\cdot cm}^{-2}\), which is smaller than \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) \((321\ \mathrm{mV})\) and slightly worse than \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) \((258\ \mathrm{mV})\) (Fig. 3(b)). When the current density achieve \(50\) and \(100\ \mathrm{mA\cdot cm}^{-2}\), the overpotentials required by \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) is much lower than \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) and \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\), suggesting that \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) has the prospect to become a candidate for industrial water oxidation. As shown in Fig. 3(c), \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) exhibit a faster electron transfer process reflected in the corresponding Tafel slope. Interestingly, both \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) \((55.8\ \mathrm{mV\cdot dec}^{-1})\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) \((81.2\ \mathrm{mV\cdot dec}^{-1})\) have a smaller Tafel slope than \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) \((83.3\ \mathrm{mV\cdot dec}^{-1})\), indicating that \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles encapsulated by the S, N co-incorporated carbon nanosheets possess sufficiently mass transport and easily electron transfer than \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) [18]. We also compared the overpotential and Tafel slope of \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) with the previous reported Co-based electrocatalysts (Fig. 3(d)), which presents \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) is much better than the most of Co-based electrocatalysts for OER catalysis in \(1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH}\) electrolyte [14, 17, 19, 30, 41, 46–55]. Furthermore, the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) yield was evaluate by drainage gas gathering method (Fig. S14 in the ESM). The \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) yield is \(0.25\ \mathrm{mL\cdot cm}^{-2}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) at \(1.603\ \mathrm{V}\), and the Faraday efficiency is \(105.3\%\), suggesting that \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) is a promising candidate to promote water splitting.
To clarify the relationship between active surface area and intrinsic electro-oxidation activity, electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) was measured (Fig. S15 in the ESM). The ECSA of \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) is \(33.3\ \mathrm{mF\cdot cm}^{-2}\), which is much bigger than that of \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) \((26.5\ \mathrm{mF\cdot cm}^{-2})\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) \((12.4\ \mathrm{mF\cdot cm}^{-2})\) (Fig. 3(e)). Whereas, the current density normalized by ECSA still has the same trend as the current density obtained by the geometric surface area (Fig. 3(f)), suggesting that the intrinsic electro-oxidation activity of the S, N co-incorporated carbon nanosheets encapsulating \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles has the capacity to better than commercial \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) under a suitable overpotentials. This phenomenon also illustrated that the number of active sites is not the main reason for its faster reaction kinetics.
Meanwhile, the ECSA of \(\mathrm{Fe-Co_{9}S_{8}@S N C}\) is more than twice that of \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\), which can be attributed to the smaller \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles and high doping proportion of graphitic N in the Fe- \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\). Generally, the intrinsic electro-oxidation activity also depends on the conductivity of electrocatalyst. Compared the Nyquist plots of \(\mathrm{Fe-Co_{9}S_{8}@S N C}\) with \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) at \(1.503\;\mathrm{V}\), Fe- \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) shows a smaller semicircle (Fig. 3(g)), confirming that \(\mathrm{Fe-Co_{9}S_{8}@S N C}\) possesses lower charge transfer resistance. Furthermore, the appropriate affinity of \(\mathrm{Fe-Co_{9}S_{8}@S N C}\) for OER can be reflected in the corresponding Bodes plots (Fig. S16 in the ESM). The smaller and right shift frequency peak could be attributed to the milder adsorption of the reactant and desorption of the oxidation product [23]. To evaluate the catalytic kinetics barriers of \(\mathrm{Fe-Co_{9}S_{8}@S N C}\) for water electro-oxidation process, the influence of the temperature on the performance of OER was studied (Fig. S17 in the ESM) [56]. Expectedly, the current density more rapidly responses at ascended temperature for these catalysts in OER process [30]. As show in Fig. 3(h), by means of the Arrhenius relationship, the electrochemical activation energies for \(\mathrm{Fe-Co_{9}S_{8}@S N C}\) are calculated as 39.05, while the value of \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) and \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) are 62.94 and \(35.35\;\mathrm{kJ}\cdot\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\), respectively. Noticeably, Fe- \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) present an approximately apparent barrier value with \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\), which is much smaller than \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\), suggesting the doped Fe atom can efficiently reduce the kinetic barriers of to \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) to appear similar activity of \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\). To further examine the durability, the stability of \(\mathrm{Fe-Co_{9}S_{8}@S N C}\) is evaluated by chronoamperometric test at the potential of \(1.553\;\mathrm{V}\), which displays no significant damping of the large current density (Fig. 3(i)). In addition, as an emerging efficient catalyst for OER, sulfides are likely oxidized during OER reactions. Therefore, the structure of \(\mathrm{Fe-Co_{9}S_{8}@S N C}\) after chronoamperometric test
were investigated. As confirmed by HRETM and SEM images (Figs. 4(a) and 4(b) and Fig. S18 in the ESM), the assembled structure of \(\mathrm{Fe-Co_{9}S_{8}@S N C}\) have been well-reserved during the OER process. From XRD patterns and Raman spectrum observation (Figs. 4(c) and 4(d)), no obvious new peak appeared after OER, suggesting the crystal structure robustness of \(\mathrm{Fe-Co_{9}S_{8}@S N C}\). However, XPS characterization shows the Co 2p and Fe 2p peaks after OER have a positive shift compared to the pristine Fe- \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) (Fig. 4(f) and Fig. S19 in the ESM), indicating that the metal sites of \(\mathrm{Fe-Co_{9}S_{8}@S N C}\) were in activated state under the condition of the well-retained morphology and crystal structure after OER. Besides, \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) present a similar durability for OER (Figs. S20 and S21 in the ESM). As proposed in Deng's work, the encased carbon layer could act as armor to steady against air oxidation and catalytic stability [26, 28, 29]. The lower overpotential, smaller Tafel slope, larger ECSA, enhanced conductivity, suitable apparent activation energy and good durability all manifest Fe-doping play a vital role in regulating the electrochemical environment of \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles encapsulated by S, N co-incorporated in carbon nanosheets. Moreover, a \(\mathrm{Pt}||\mathrm{Fe-Co_{9}S_{8}@S N C}\) electrochemical configuration was constructed to evaluate the performance of overall water splitting. As shown in Fig. S22 in the ESM, \(\mathrm{Pt}||\mathrm{Fe-Co_{9}S_{8}@S N C}\) exhibit a small cell voltage of \(1.556\;\mathrm{V}\) to achieve the current density of \(10\;\mathrm{mA\cdot cm}^{-2}\) for overall water splitting, which is much better than \(\mathrm{Pt}||\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) (1.621 V). Therefore, these results suggested that \(\mathrm{Fe-Co_{9}S_{8}@S N C}\) may be an promising anode electrocatalysts for water electrolysis.
To elucidate the essential mechanism of outstanding OER activity by Fe doping, the local charge density and d-band center (Fe) were inspected. Considering that the d orbital electron
of the transition metal atom determines the adsorption capacity of the oxygen intermediate during the catalytic process [57], the density of states of the cobalt atom before and after doping were calculated. First, high local DOS values across the Fermi level present promising sign for metallic electronic properties for \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\), which could ensures a fast charge migration during the electrocatalytic pathway [14]. The contribution of nonorbital to the density of states were further considered. As shown in Fig. 5(a) and Fig. S23 in the ESM, the \(d_{xz}\)-orbital density around the Fermi level of Co atomic occurred a significant decrease after Fe doping. Combined with the XPS result, this weaken of density may be resulting from the partial charge transfer from \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) [58, 59]. This local charge density engineering results in a poor of electrons around the Co
atom, which may lead to a reduction in the adsorption energy of oxygen intermediates, and hence facilitating the desorption of oxygen intermediates from the surface of the catalysts for facile reaction kinetics [60]. Furthermore, the d-band center model would be a good descriptor of the adsorbate–metal interaction [61]. The d-band center \((E_{\mathrm{d}})\) of \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) relative to \(E_{\mathrm{f}}\) are calculated to be \(-2.044\) and \(-2.108\;\;\mathrm{eV}\), respectively (Fig. 5(b)), which clearly illustrated that the d-band center has a conspicuous downshift by Fe doping. It also suggests that the antibonding energy states are lowered and the interaction between the adsorbate and surface is weakened [62, 63]. Therefore, the changes of the local charge density and the downshift of the d-band center provide the intrinsic elucidation that Fe doping decreases the adsorption energy of
oxygen intermediates and meanwhile facilitates the desorption of oxygen intermediates from the catalyst surface during OER process [52, 62, 64]. Integrated the above structure information, the essence of enhanced OER activity of doped Fe can be summarized to the following aspects (Fig. 5(c)): (i) connected nanosheet by nanoparticles retained rich access for permeation of electrolyte and precipitation of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) bubbles during OER process; (ii) the outer carbon layer provides additional catalytic activity while acting as armor to keep the structure stable; (iii) The change of the local charge density and downshift of the d-band center after Fe doping facilitates the desorption of oxygen intermediates.
## 4 Conclusion
In summary, focusing on the multi-scale regulation for efficient water oxidation, the dense, uniform \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles encapsulated by S, N co-incorporating carbon nanosheets were successfully obtained through the fast thermal treatment of Co-MOFs. The strategy of topological chemical transformation endows the catalyst system with abundant voids between \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles and strong coupling of \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles/carbon layer. In this regard, the rich porosity contributed the good infiltration of electrolyte and slippage of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) bubbles, guaranteeing smooth mass transfer during OER. Meanwhile, the strong coupling of \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles/carbon layer can protect \(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) nanoparticles from erosion on the basis of providing additional catalytic activity. More importantly, at the atomic scale, the modulation of the local charge density and the d-band center by Fe doping facilitates desorption of oxygen intermediates for enhancing the intrinsic activity. Electrochemical measurements revealed that the \(\mathrm{Fe-Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNC}\) displays an ultralow overpotential of \(273\;\mathrm{mV}\) at \(0\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), which is even close to the benchmark \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) catalyst. Profoundly, the multiscale coordinated regulation will provide a guide to design high-efficiency electrocatalysts.
